# KNOWN GAP — READING through an EXPLICIT `&` BORROW PARAMETER DESTROYS the
# caller's value. `gg check` is clean; the read succeeds; the caller is gutted.
#
#     void peek(Option[String] &o):
#         print(o.unwrap())          # prints correctly
#     ...
#     peek(&v)
#     print(v.unwrap())              # trap[T_UnwrapNone], rc 101
#
# Observed: the string prints from inside `peek`, then the caller's `v` traps
# as `None`. The user wrote the borrow sigil explicitly and a READ still
# consumed the value.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE SHARPEST CELL OF THE BORROW-PROVENANCE AXIS. `t0036` records
# the same mechanism for a value borrowed out of a COLLECTION
# (`d.get(0).unwrap()`); this cell reaches it through a PARAMETER borrow, with
# no collection accessor anywhere in the program. A rule whose subject is
# "collection borrows" has no subject that covers this (Core #15e Q4 -- a
# category error, which no widening of the collection rule fixes).
#
# SIBLING CELL, same axis, filed together and WORSE: `self` in an `equip`
# method -- see `read_through_equip_self_destroys_receiver.gg`, where the trap
# fires INSIDE the method on its own first read, i.e. the receiver binding
# gutted the value before the body ran.
#
# ggdef adjudicates AGAINST production here: the definitional oracle prints the
# value twice, which is the intended behaviour asserted below.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints the string TWICE, rc 0, on both backends.
# Measured 2026-08-27 at HEAD by R45 Track D brief-review pass 2;
# orchestrator-verified.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void peek(Option[String] &o):
    print(o.unwrap())

void main():
    Option[String] v = Some(mk("aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa", "bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb"))
    peek(&v)
    print(v.unwrap())
